1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:02,520
Welcome to the Knowledge Architecture
Workbench.

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:08,160
Every day, engineers ask seemingly simple
questions about complex products.

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:12,860
Which exact component was installed in
this vehicle when a failure occurred?

4
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:18,300
Yet, before a real-world question can
become useful knowledge, data must pass

5
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:23,640
through several architectural layers, from
domain concepts and formal logic down to

6
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:25,100
physical database tables.

7
00:00:25,820 --> 00:00:30,120
This interactive tool demonstrates how
different graph technologies handle that

8
00:00:30,120 --> 00:00:34,480
journey, exposing the hidden traps in
designing knowledge management systems.

9
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:38,940
You can explore pre-recorded edge cases,
like this guided tour,

10
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:44,460
or use the open sandbox to build your own
structures and switch storage modes on the fly.

11
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:48,780
Let's see the sandbox in action.

12
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:53,220
Using the operations toolbar, we act as
the domain engineer.

13
00:00:54,880 --> 00:01:00,280
Let's install a new component, Pump_B,
into System_A.

14
00:01:01,380 --> 00:01:04,040
Look at the BOM tree structure on the top
right.

15
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:10,600
Regardless of the underlying database, the engineer's 
viewpoint remains a clean, familiar hierarchy.

16
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:20,220
Now, let's advance the clock to T equals 1
and report a 2 millimeter crack on Part_A.

17
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:22,540
Watch the canvas.

18
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:28,740
In this naive graph mode, where we model
data without strict ontological rules, the

19
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:33,300
defect simply overwrites the node's
label, destroying its prior history.

20
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:40,940
If we now ask what Part_A looked like at T
equals 0, the database will falsely claim

21
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:42,240
it was born defective.

22
00:01:44,660 --> 00:01:49,940
Let's step forward to T equals 2 and
dismantle the broken Part_A.

23
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:56,880
Now, let's pull the architectural levers
at the top left without losing our history.

24
00:01:57,900 --> 00:01:59,380
Switching to BFO,

25
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,240
3D continuance, keeps the physical part
unchanged through time, attaching the

26
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:07,720
crack as a separate dependent quality
node.

27
00:02:10,199 --> 00:02:18,380
Switching to ISO 15926, 4D extents, treats
objects as spatiotemporal worms.

28
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,140
Notice how the crack at T equals 1 forced
the system to slice both Part_A and

29
00:02:26,140 --> 00:02:33,220
System_A into new immutable temporal
states, preserving a mathematically pristine audit trail.

30
00:02:35,560 --> 00:02:42,480
Finally, look at the bottom right panels
as we toggle between LPG and W3CRDF.

31
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:50,460
Under the Bonnet translates our live graph
into raw database tables, or RDF triples,

32
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:56,440
in real time, while Database Query
reveals the exact Cypher or SPARQL code

33
00:02:56,440 --> 00:02:58,860
required to extract knowledge at time T.

34
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:05,600
Feel free to replay any step, load another
scenario, or experiment with the controls yourself.
